@meta
id: 11-06
title: One change, not two
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-design
par: 38
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl+D ten times, or find-and-replace
open: queue-export.txt

@brief
The nightly queue export writes its status column in lower case. The
ledger importer only accepts upper case, so ten rows need `pending`
turned into `PENDING`.

There are two obvious ways to replace a word: delete it and then type the
new one, or change it in one go. They produce the same ten characters.
Only one of them leaves something behind that `.` can use.

Do the first row, then find out which route you picked by pressing `.` on
the second.

@keys
| `ciw` | change the inner word — delete it and drop straight into insert |
| `Esc` | ends the change |
| `.` | repeat the whole change: the delete and the typing together |
| `dw` then `i` | two changes. `.` repeats only the second one |

@hints
Whatever you do to the first row, the repeat will replay exactly one
command. Make sure that command is the entire edit.
--
`c` is `d` and `i` fused into a single command, and a single command is a
single change. `ciw` from anywhere inside the word.
--
`ciwPENDING` then `Esc`, then `j.` nine times. The cursor lands on the
last letter of `PENDING`, which is still inside the next `pending` down,
so `ciw` finds its own boundaries and you never have to aim.

@solution
    ciw PENDING <Esc>   then  j.  nine times

A change is one command that alters the buffer, from the moment it starts
to the moment you are back in normal mode. `ciw` is one command. `dw`
followed by `iPENDING` and `Esc` is two, and `.` only ever holds the last
one — so the repeat would insert `PENDING` in front of the word it was
supposed to replace, and you would be undoing your way back out.

The same rule sets the size of a change, and that cuts the other way.
Everything you type between entering insert mode and pressing `Esc` is one
change, however long. Sit in insert mode for two minutes writing a whole
function and vim dutifully remembers all of it as a single blunt lump —
technically repeatable, useless in practice, and it is why people say
their `.` never does anything helpful.

Small, complete, self-aiming changes. That is the whole discipline:

- one command, so the repeat holds all of it
- ended with `Esc` promptly, so it stays small
- built on a text object, so it lands correctly wherever the cursor is

Note the third one paying off above. `ciw` works from any character in
the word, so a bare `j` is enough to set up the next repeat. `cw` would
have needed the cursor on the first letter and cost you a `0` every line.

